# ao3-source-code-import

## Purpose

Convert source code from local git repositories (starting with world-edit-to-execute) into a format suitable for programmatic upload to Archive of Our Own (AO3). Use AO3 as a preservation layer for code - treating source as literary artifact.

## Core Philosophy

- Source code is text. Text belongs in archives.
- Version control through replacement: old versions deprecated, new versions uploaded.
- Nature's demand: preserve the work, make it findable, let it exist beyond local machines.

## Primary Workflow

1. Read source repository (world-edit-to-execute as initial target)
2. Transform code files into AO3-compatible HTML format
   - Preserve syntax structure
   - Include metadata (commit history, file structure, purpose)
   - Format as readable "chapters" or sections
3. Authenticate with AO3
4. Upload as a "work" with appropriate tags
5. On subsequent runs: update existing work, mark previous as deprecated or replace

## Key Considerations

- AO3 rate limits and terms of service
- Code formatting that survives AO3's HTML sanitization
- Meaningful tagging (Original Work, Software, etc.)
- Chapter organization (by file? by module? by phase?)
- Commit message as author's notes
- README as summary

## Target Repository

Primary: /home/ritz/programming/ai-stuff/world-edit-to-execute/
Future: Any git repository with a compatible structure

## Output Format

A single AO3 "work" containing:
- Summary derived from project vision/README
- Chapters organized by logical code sections
- Code formatted with <pre> blocks or similar
- Issue history as supplementary material
- Git log as historical record
